We further investigated the transport characteristics of the LDH, C/LDH, and C/LDH/S by electrochemical impedance spectroscopy to understand their electrochemical behavior. Equivalent series resistance of ${\mathrm{C/LDH/S}}$ is found to be $2.21\Omega$ , smaller than that for C/LDH $_{(2.88~\Omega)}$ and LDH $\left(3.53~\Omega\right)$ as presented in Figure 3e. Smaller charge transfer resistance of C/LDH/S, determined from the diameter of the semicircle, and improved ion diffusion, suggested by the higher angle vertical line, substantiate the enhanced electrochemical properties of C/LDH/S. Furthermore, LDH derived from calcined ZIF-67 (C/LDH) resulted in augmented supercapacitive properties compared to the pristine LDH. This improvement can be attributed to enlarged interlayer spacing of C/LDH and increased amorphous carbon content, which facilitate electrolyte penetration for fast diffusion and reaction, leading to higher reaction kinetics.[16c]
An asymmetric supercapacitor device with two-electrode configuration (see the details in Figure S22, Supporting Information) was assembled using $\mathrm{C/LDH/S}$ and carbon nanotubes (CNTs) as positive and negative electrodes, respectively. A specific capacitance of $194\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ is calculated at $4\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ (Figure S22c, Supporting Information), which decreases to $151\ensuremath{\mathrm{~F~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at $12\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ , showing a superior rate capability of $77.8\%$ (Figure 3f). Furthermore, $\mathrm{C/LDH/S//CNTs}$ exhibits a high stability with $90.9\%$ retention of the initial capacitance (Figure S22e, Supporting Information) when cycled for $10\ 000$ cycles at $8\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ . The cell also delivers a high energy density of $39\mathrm{\:Wh\:kg^{-1}}$ at the lowest power density of $2.4\ensuremath{~\mathrm{kW}~\mathrm{kg}^{-1}}$ when the current density is $4\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , and it can hold 30.1 Wh $\mathrm{kg^{-1}}$ even at the highest power density of $7.4\ensuremath{~\mathrm{kW}~}\ensuremath{\mathrm{kg}^{-1}}$ (Figure S22f, Supporting Information).
